1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,397
In the summer of nineteen forty-seven, a rancher in New Mexico found a field of strange wreckage.

2
00:00:07,397 --> 00:00:11,766
Foil, rubber, tape and sticks, scattered across his pasture.

3
00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:17,646
Days later, the United States Army told the world it had captured a flying disc.

4
00:00:17,646 --> 00:00:21,233
The next day, it said the disc was a weather balloon.

5
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:26,717
For eighty years, people have argued about what really fell at Roswell.

6
00:00:26,717 --> 00:00:27,424
A balloon?

7
00:00:27,424 --> 00:00:28,908
A secret spy program?

8
00:00:28,908 --> 00:00:32,866
Or a crashed spacecraft, with bodies, hidden ever since?

9
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:39,643
Tonight we tell this case the way the world heard it: as one story, printed again and again.

10
00:00:39,643 --> 00:00:40,671
Seven editions.

11
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:42,866
Each time, the headline changed.

12
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:47,847
For every edition, we check what was new, and whether the evidence held.

13
00:00:47,847 --> 00:00:50,167
At the end, we print the corrections.

14
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:51,233
Stop the presses.

15
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:56,619
Edition one.

16
00:00:56,619 --> 00:00:59,223
July eighth, nineteen forty-seven.

17
00:00:59,223 --> 00:01:07,266
The story starts weeks earlier, in June, on a sheep ranch near Corona, run by a foreman named Mac Brazel.

18
00:01:07,733 --> 00:01:14,039
Brazel finds tinfoil, rubber, tape and thin wooden beams spread over several acres.

19
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:16,243
He has no phone and no radio.

20
00:01:16,243 --> 00:01:19,966
But that summer, America has flying saucer fever.

21
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:28,274
Since pilot Kenneth Arnold reported fast, shining objects in June, there have been more than eight hundred sightings.

22
00:01:28,274 --> 00:01:33,099
A reward of about three thousand dollars is on offer for physical proof.

23
00:01:33,566 --> 00:01:38,928
On July sixth, Brazel drives into Roswell and tells Sheriff George Wilcox.

24
00:01:38,928 --> 00:01:49,433
Wilcox calls Roswell Army Air Field, home of the five hundred and ninth Bomb Group, the only unit in the world that could deliver an atomic bomb.

25
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:58,045
Intelligence officer Major Jesse Marcel and Captain Sheridan Cavitt drive out to the ranch and gather the material.

26
00:01:58,045 --> 00:02:02,933
The base commander, Colonel William Blanchard, informs his superiors.

27
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:09,267
Then, on July eighth, public information officer Walter Haut issues a press release.

28
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:15,833
The base has gained possession of a disc, it says, through the cooperation of a local rancher.

29
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:26,575
That afternoon the Roswell Daily Record runs it across the top of the front page: R A A F captures flying saucer on ranch in Roswell region.

30
00:02:26,575 --> 00:02:31,566
A local radio station's phones jam with calls from around the world.

31
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:32,933
Edition two.

32
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:33,758
July ninth.

33
00:02:33,758 --> 00:02:36,983
The material is flown to Fort Worth, Texas.

34
00:02:36,983 --> 00:02:43,133
General Roger Ramey invites the press into his office and spreads it on the floor.

35
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:52,733
His weather officer, Irving Newton, identifies it: the remains of a weather balloon and a radar target, a kite of sticks and foil.

36
00:02:52,733 --> 00:02:57,300
Similar targets, he says, fly from about eighty weather stations.

37
00:02:57,766 --> 00:03:00,985
Major Marcel is photographed holding the foil.

38
00:03:00,985 --> 00:03:03,995
Back in Roswell, Brazel gives an interview.

39
00:03:03,995 --> 00:03:10,433
He describes rubber strips, tinfoil, paper, tape and sticks, about five pounds of it in all.

40
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:11,551
No metal.

41
00:03:11,551 --> 00:03:12,274
No engine.

42
00:03:12,274 --> 00:03:13,287
No propellers.

43
00:03:13,287 --> 00:03:17,845
But one odd detail: some of the tape had flowers printed on it.

44
00:03:17,845 --> 00:03:19,220
Remember that tape.

45
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:20,233
It comes back.

46
00:03:20,699 --> 00:03:25,719
That same day, the FBI's Dallas office sends a teletype to Washington.

47
00:03:25,719 --> 00:03:33,033
The object, it says, is hexagonal, and was hanging from a balloon about twenty feet across by a cable.

48
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:41,588
And Brazel adds a line that believers still quote: he is sure that what he found was not any weather observation balloon.

49
00:03:41,588 --> 00:03:44,663
He has found weather balloons before, he says.

50
00:03:44,663 --> 00:03:45,933
This was different.

51
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:49,574
Notice that both stories can be partly true.

52
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:53,976
Brazel was right that it was not an ordinary weather balloon.

53
00:03:53,976 --> 00:03:56,718
Ramey was right that it was a balloon.

54
00:03:56,718 --> 00:03:58,666
The question is: what kind?

55
00:03:59,133 --> 00:04:01,194
Within days the story dies.

56
00:04:01,194 --> 00:04:06,691
A crashed disc in Idaho turns out to be a hoax built from jukebox parts.

57
00:04:06,691 --> 00:04:09,363
Roswell disappears from the papers.

58
00:04:09,363 --> 00:04:10,966
For thirty-one years.

59
00:04:11,433 --> 00:04:12,484
Edition three.

60
00:04:12,484 --> 00:04:14,210
Nineteen seventy-eight.

61
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:21,566
A nuclear physicist turned UFO researcher, Stanton Friedman, tracks down the retired Jesse Marcel.

62
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:28,600
Marcel says the weather balloon was a cover story, and that he now believes the debris was not from Earth.

63
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:33,000
He describes a foil that could be crumpled, and would spring back flat.

64
00:04:33,466 --> 00:04:40,535
In nineteen eighty, on national television, he says the general told the press it was a weather balloon.

65
00:04:40,535 --> 00:04:43,866
Of course, Marcel adds, we both knew differently.

66
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:50,957
His son, ten years old in nineteen forty-seven, remembers a small beam with purple hieroglyphics.

67
00:04:50,957 --> 00:04:53,279
But here is the flower tape again.

68
00:04:53,279 --> 00:05:01,133
The Air Force later showed the balloons used tape from a New York toy maker, printed with abstract flower patterns.

69
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:02,993
Then comes the book.

70
00:05:02,993 --> 00:05:09,336
In October nineteen eighty, Charles Berlitz and William Moore publish The Roswell Incident.

71
00:05:09,336 --> 00:05:11,287
A craft struck by lightning.

72
00:05:11,287 --> 00:05:13,100
And something new: bodies.

73
00:05:13,566 --> 00:05:22,554
The bodies come from a second-hand story, told by a man who had died years earlier, set about a hundred and fifty miles away.

74
00:05:22,554 --> 00:05:25,286
Marcel himself never mentioned bodies.

75
00:05:25,286 --> 00:05:25,933
Not once.

76
00:05:26,399 --> 00:05:37,600
And Brazel's daughter, fourteen at the time, remembers what she helped pick up: waxed paper, aluminum-like foil, and tape that showed pastel flowers when held to the light.

77
00:05:38,066 --> 00:05:39,047
Edition four.

78
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:41,764
Nineteen eighty-four to ninety-four.

79
00:05:41,764 --> 00:05:42,971
The story grows.

80
00:05:42,971 --> 00:05:54,366
In December nineteen eighty-four, a TV producer receives an anonymous envelope: film of secret documents describing a committee called Majestic Twelve.

81
00:05:54,833 --> 00:05:57,533
The documents fall apart under scrutiny.

82
00:05:57,533 --> 00:06:04,283
A President Truman signature is identical to one on a real letter from October nineteen forty-seven.

83
00:06:04,283 --> 00:06:11,033
Every recipient connects back to William Moore, who later confessed to feeding fakes to researchers.

84
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:24,266
In nineteen eighty-nine, a former Roswell mortician, Glenn Dennis, describes calls from the base asking about small caskets, and a nurse who told him she had seen an alien autopsy.

85
00:06:24,733 --> 00:06:27,085
Researchers search for the nurse.

86
00:06:27,085 --> 00:06:27,798
No record.

87
00:06:27,798 --> 00:06:29,723
Dennis gives a second name.

88
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:30,863
No record again.

89
00:06:30,863 --> 00:06:34,000
In the end, he admits he made up both names.

90
00:06:34,466 --> 00:06:35,840
It does not matter.

91
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:41,265
That year, twenty-eight million people watch Roswell on Unsolved Mysteries.

92
00:06:41,265 --> 00:06:42,206
Books follow.

93
00:06:42,206 --> 00:06:45,533
One sells a hundred and sixty thousand copies.

94
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,650
Some three hundred people come forward.

95
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:54,698
Yet, as one historian wrote, the more information arrived, the less clear Roswell became.

96
00:06:54,698 --> 00:07:00,066
Of all those people, only about two dozen could plausibly have seen any debris.

97
00:07:00,533 --> 00:07:02,048
The crash site moves.

98
00:07:02,048 --> 00:07:04,718
The number of saucers goes up to two.

99
00:07:04,718 --> 00:07:07,893
The aliens grow to eight, two of them alive.

100
00:07:07,893 --> 00:07:11,285
One witness even recalls eleven golden helmets.

101
00:07:11,285 --> 00:07:14,100
The researchers split into rival camps.

102
00:07:14,566 --> 00:07:16,380
But one admission is real.

103
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:25,103
In nineteen ninety-one, General Thomas DuBose, who had posed with the debris, confirms the weather balloon was a cover story.

104
00:07:25,103 --> 00:07:27,266
The question is: covering what?

105
00:07:27,733 --> 00:07:28,688
Edition five.

106
00:07:28,688 --> 00:07:30,232
Nineteen ninety-four.

107
00:07:30,232 --> 00:07:37,801
After a congressman, Steven Schiff of New Mexico, demands answers, the Air Force searches its archives.

108
00:07:37,801 --> 00:07:41,843
Its report says: the weather balloon story was a cover.

109
00:07:41,843 --> 00:07:43,166
For Project Mogul.

110
00:07:43,633 --> 00:07:45,136
Mogul was top secret.

111
00:07:45,136 --> 00:07:53,440
The idea: Soviet atomic tests would send low sound waves around the planet through a channel high in the atmosphere.

112
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:56,733
Balloons carrying microphones could hear them.

113
00:07:57,199 --> 00:08:11,100
On June fourth, nineteen forty-seven, a New York University team launches flight number four from Alamogordo: a chain of balloons, radar reflectors and equipment, about six hundred feet long.

114
00:08:11,566 --> 00:08:15,878
They lose track of it within seventeen miles of Brazel's ranch.

115
00:08:15,878 --> 00:08:17,795
The flight is not recovered.

116
00:08:17,795 --> 00:08:24,366
Mogul's records do not prove its exact path, but it was heading the right way at the right time.

117
00:08:24,833 --> 00:08:26,627
Now the odd details fit.

118
00:08:26,627 --> 00:08:29,469
Foil and sticks: the radar reflectors.

119
00:08:29,469 --> 00:08:33,731
Grey rubber and waxed paper: the balloons and their gear.

120
00:08:33,731 --> 00:08:36,273
Flower tape: the toy maker's tape.

121
00:08:36,273 --> 00:08:41,433
And a hexagon hanging from a balloon: exactly what the FBI described.

122
00:08:41,899 --> 00:08:52,233
Captain Cavitt, who gathered the debris beside Marcel, gives a sworn statement: I thought at the time and think so now, that this debris was from a crashed balloon.

123
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:55,786
Then the General Accounting Office reports.

124
00:08:55,786 --> 00:09:09,926
It finds a red flag: Roswell base records, all the administrative files from nineteen forty-five to forty-nine, and all outgoing messages from late nineteen forty-six to forty-nine, were destroyed.

125
00:09:09,926 --> 00:09:12,366
Nobody recorded who, when, or why.

126
00:09:12,833 --> 00:09:14,584
Believers see a cover-up.

127
00:09:14,584 --> 00:09:18,645
And there was one: the Army really did hide what it found.

128
00:09:18,645 --> 00:09:28,100
But the records cover years of routine paperwork, and the two documents from nineteen forty-seven that survive both describe a balloon.

129
00:09:28,566 --> 00:09:29,500
Edition six.

130
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,135
Nineteen ninety-five.

131
00:09:31,135 --> 00:09:43,200
Just as the Air Force says balloon, a British entrepreneur, Ray Santilli, releases black and white film of an alien autopsy, supposedly shot after Roswell.

132
00:09:43,666 --> 00:09:46,470
More than twenty million people watch it.

133
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:50,299
The cameraman he named had never served in the military.

134
00:09:50,299 --> 00:09:52,966
Hollywood effects experts call it fake.

135
00:09:53,433 --> 00:10:00,996
In two thousand six, Santilli admits it: the film was staged, shot on a set built in a London living room.

136
00:10:00,996 --> 00:10:05,135
He still claims he was restoring real footage he once saw.

137
00:10:05,135 --> 00:10:07,133
No one has ever produced it.

138
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:14,968
In nineteen ninety-seven, fifty years after Roswell, the Air Force releases a second report, Case Closed.

139
00:10:14,968 --> 00:10:20,933
It proposes that many stories of alien bodies were really memories of something else.

140
00:10:21,399 --> 00:10:28,677
In the nineteen-fifties, the Air Force dropped human-shaped test dummies from high balloons over New Mexico.

141
00:10:28,677 --> 00:10:37,033
They were bald, wore flight suits, and were collected on stretchers, sometimes in insulated bags that looked like body bags.

142
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,418
But the timing is a problem.

143
00:10:39,418 --> 00:10:42,913
Those drops began years after nineteen forty-seven.

144
00:10:42,913 --> 00:10:47,162
The Air Force argued that, decades later, memories had merged.

145
00:10:47,162 --> 00:10:52,233
Critics called that a stretch, and on this point they had a fair argument.

146
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:59,250
The same year, a retired officer published a book claiming Roswell technology won the Cold War.

147
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:03,939
A senator who had written its foreword demanded his name be removed.

148
00:11:03,939 --> 00:11:06,766
He had been told it was a different book.

149
00:11:07,233 --> 00:11:08,222
Edition seven.

150
00:11:08,222 --> 00:11:09,000
The latest.

151
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,827
December twenty-sixth, two thousand two.

152
00:11:11,827 --> 00:11:20,733
Walter Haut, the officer who wrote the flying disc press release, signs a sealed statement, to be opened only after his death.

153
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:26,065
Published in two thousand seven, it says the colonel took him into a hangar.

154
00:11:26,065 --> 00:11:33,299
There he saw a metallic egg-shaped craft, twelve to fifteen feet long, and two small bodies under a canvas sheet.

155
00:11:33,766 --> 00:11:38,026
And in October twenty twenty-six, it is back in the news.

156
00:11:38,026 --> 00:11:47,666
The Pentagon's seventh release of UFO files includes the same affidavit, redacted, inside a two thousand eight contractor report.

157
00:11:48,133 --> 00:11:49,619
So how strong is it?

158
00:11:49,619 --> 00:11:54,746
It is the testimony of a man in his eighties, fifty-five years later.

159
00:11:54,746 --> 00:11:59,725
The text was drafted by a UFO researcher and emailed to his family.

160
00:11:59,725 --> 00:12:03,366
He signed it at the UFO museum he had co-founded.

161
00:12:03,833 --> 00:12:12,538
In an earlier sworn statement, in nineteen ninety-three, Haut described issuing the press release, and nothing about a craft or bodies.

162
00:12:12,538 --> 00:12:18,600
His own daughter said that for years, he told her only one thing: I put out the press release.

163
00:12:19,066 --> 00:12:26,505
Meanwhile, the Pentagon's own UFO office reviewed every official investigation since nineteen forty-five.

164
00:12:26,505 --> 00:12:36,000
In twenty twenty-four it reported no evidence of extraterrestrial technology, and pointed back to the Roswell reports of the nineties.

165
00:12:36,466 --> 00:12:40,982
Today, Roswell welcomes around ninety thousand visitors a year.

166
00:12:40,982 --> 00:12:43,348
Its street lamps have alien eyes.

167
00:12:43,348 --> 00:12:46,216
The story became bigger than the debris.

168
00:12:46,216 --> 00:12:48,366
Time to print the corrections.

169
00:12:48,833 --> 00:12:49,811
The corrections.

170
00:12:49,811 --> 00:12:52,500
Seven editions, scored against the evidence.

171
00:12:52,966 --> 00:12:57,165
The debris was foil, rubber, paper, tape and sticks: evidence.

172
00:12:57,165 --> 00:13:02,311
It matches a Project Mogul balloon train, down to the flower tape: evidence.

173
00:13:02,311 --> 00:13:08,134
The weather balloon story was a cover, admitted by DuBose and the Air Force: evidence.

174
00:13:08,134 --> 00:13:12,333
Mogul flight four was lost near the ranch that June: evidence.

175
00:13:12,799 --> 00:13:19,792
Alien bodies: no evidence, only late, second-hand and contradictory stories, and an invented nurse.

176
00:13:19,792 --> 00:13:23,889
The autopsy film and the Majestic Twelve papers: debunked.

177
00:13:23,889 --> 00:13:28,833
Exactly which flight, and why base records were destroyed: still open.

178
00:13:29,299 --> 00:13:30,145
The verdict.

179
00:13:30,145 --> 00:13:35,497
Something really did crash at Roswell, and the Army really did lie about it.

180
00:13:35,497 --> 00:13:37,750
But the secret was not a saucer.

181
00:13:37,750 --> 00:13:43,666
It was a Cold War listening device, a string of balloons built to hear Soviet bombs.

182
00:13:44,133 --> 00:13:52,068
The bodies, the craft and the autopsy were added later, edition by edition, by people who were never on that ranch.

183
00:13:52,068 --> 00:13:54,966
The real debris weighed about five pounds.

184
00:13:55,433 --> 00:13:58,434
Roswell is not proof that we were visited.

185
00:13:58,434 --> 00:14:05,366
It is proof of how a story can outgrow its facts, and how a secret, kept badly, becomes a legend.

186
00:14:05,833 --> 00:14:07,888
Which edition fooled you longest?

187
00:14:07,888 --> 00:14:12,433
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next case put to the test.
